---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import QuickAnswer from "../components/QuickAnswer.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Power Stations",
    "url": "/best-portable-power-station/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Jackery Power Stations",
    "url": "/best-jackery-power-station/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Power Station for Home Backup",
    "url": "/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/"
  },
  {
    "title": "EcoFlow vs Bluetti",
    "url": "/ecoflow-vs-bluetti/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-03", modified: "2026-11-03" });
const title = "7 Best Anker Power Stations in 2026: Every SOLIX by Job";
const description =
  "Seven Anker SOLIX power stations from the C300X to the F3800 Plus, compared on capacity, output, charging and expansion, with a clear pick for each use case.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-c1000",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX C1000 Portable Power Station",
    "category": "1,056Wh LFP, 1,800W, Expandable",
    "badge": "Best Overall Anker",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Full wall recharge in under an hour (UltraFast rating)",
      "1,800W output runs most single household appliances",
      "Expands to about 2kWh with the BP1000 battery",
      "LFP cells and a five-year warranty",
      "Up to 600W solar input"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 28 pounds, heavier than the lightest 1kWh rivals",
      "Owners commonly report loud fans during fast charging",
      "120V only"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Anker most households should buy: fast to refill, big enough for a fridge, small enough to take camping.",
    "description": "The C1000 is the center of the SOLIX lineup and the one we recommend to most readers. Anker rates it at 1,056 watt-hours of LFP storage and 1,800 watts of continuous output, with a SurgePad mode for heating appliances that briefly pull more. That covers a refrigerator for most of a day alongside a modem and lights, or a coffee maker and a microwave one at a time. Its standout feature is recharge speed: in UltraFast mode Anker rates a full refill from a wall outlet in under an hour, which is exactly what you want when power flickers on and off through a storm. It accepts up to 600 watts of solar, enough for two or three portable panels, and the BP1000 expansion battery roughly doubles its capacity if you outgrow it. You may also see a C1000X listing; compare the specs, and if they match, buy whichever is cheaper."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-c1000-gen-2",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 Portable Power Station",
    "category": "1,024Wh LFP, 2,000W, Faster Charging",
    "badge": "Fastest-Charging Anker",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "2,000W output, more headroom than the original C1000",
      "Even faster wall recharging than the first generation",
      "Smaller and lighter than the original by Anker's figures",
      "LFP cells and app control"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Slightly less capacity than the original C1000",
      "Accessory compatibility differs from the first generation",
      "Usually priced above the original"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it over the original C1000 when the price gap is small; the extra output and faster charging are real upgrades.",
    "description": "The C1000 Gen 2 is a ground-up redesign rather than a cosmetic refresh. Anker rates it at 1,024 watt-hours of LFP storage, a little less than the original, but raises continuous output to 2,000 watts, which gives more margin for kitchen appliances, power tools and refrigerator start-up surges. Charging is faster again: Anker markets an even shorter wall recharge than the already quick first-generation model, so a short window of grid power during an outage refills most of the battery. It is also more compact by Anker's figures. The trade-offs are modest. Capacity dips slightly, it usually costs more than the original while both are on sale, and accessories such as expansion batteries are not necessarily shared between generations, so check compatibility before you plan a bigger system. If the two are within a small price band, the Gen 2 is the better buy; if the original is on a deep discount, it still does the job very well."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-c300x",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX C300X Portable Power Station",
    "category": "288Wh LFP, 300W, About 9 lb",
    "badge": "Best Small Anker",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Light enough to carry one-handed or pack in a backpack",
      "High-output USB-C for laptops",
      "AC outlets for a fan, lights or a CPAP",
      "LFP cells for a long cycle life"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "300W AC output cannot run kitchen appliances or a fridge",
      "Too large to fly with"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right buy for devices, day trips and a CPAP for a night; not a refrigerator backup.",
    "description": "The C300X sits right where a power bank turns into a power station, and for device charging that is the sweet spot. Anker rates it at 288 watt-hours of LFP storage and 300 watts of AC output, with a high-wattage USB-C port that can charge most laptops at full speed. In practical terms that is dozens of phone charges, several laptop refills, or a night or two of a CPAP without humidification, in a case of about 9 pounds. It is the model to buy for a day at the beach, tailgating, a hiking campsite or a home-office emergency kit. What it cannot do is run anything with a heating element or a compressor, so do not expect it to keep a refrigerator cold. Make sure the listing says C300X rather than C300 DC, because the DC version has no AC outlets at all."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-c800-plus",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX C800 Plus Portable Power Station",
    "category": "768Wh LFP, 1,200W, Built-in Light",
    "badge": "Best Anker for Camping",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Built-in pop-up camping light",
      "1,200W output runs a 12V fridge, a kettle or small tools",
      "Lighter than the C1000",
      "Fast wall charging"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not expandable",
      "Less margin than the C1000 for outage use"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A smart car-camping box; for home backup, spend a little more on the C1000.",
    "description": "The C800 Plus is Anker's answer to the camper who wants more than a C300X but does not want to haul a full 1kWh unit. Anker rates it at 768 watt-hours of LFP storage and 1,200 watts of continuous output, and builds in a pop-up camping light that turns the station into a lantern for the picnic table. That combination suits drive-in camping well: it runs a 12V or AC cooler for a couple of days, charges everyone's devices, and powers a kettle, a small blender or a CPAP. Recharging from the wall is fast by Anker's rating, and it accepts solar for longer trips. The limitation is that it is not expandable and has less headroom than the C1000, so as a home outage backup it is a step behind. Buy it if camping is the main job and outages are secondary; buy the C1000 if it is the other way round."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-f2000",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX F2000 Portable Power Station",
    "category": "2,048Wh LFP, 2,400W, Wheeled",
    "badge": "Best 2kWh Anker",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Twice the energy of the C1000",
      "2,400W output for larger appliances",
      "Wheels and a telescoping handle",
      "Expands to about 4kWh with Anker's matching battery"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Heavy enough that the wheels are a necessity",
      "120V only, no 240V loads",
      "Older design than the C-series Gen 2 units"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A full day of fridge and freezer in one box; the F3800 is the step up only if you need 240V.",
    "description": "Double the battery of the 1kWh class changes what a power station can do in an outage, and the F2000 is Anker's 2kWh option. It is the same unit once sold as the PowerHouse 767, and Anker rates it at 2,048 watt-hours of LFP storage and 2,400 watts of continuous output. That is enough to run a refrigerator and a chest freezer through a full day, or to power a sump pump intermittently with energy to spare for lights and internet. It rolls on wheels with a telescoping handle, which matters because it is too heavy to carry comfortably. A matching expansion battery doubles it to about 4kWh. The F2000 is 120V only, so it cannot run a 240V well pump or feed both legs of a panel; for that, look at the F3800. Its design is older than the newest C-series models, so compare it against a C1000 with an expansion battery, which can be cheaper and more flexible."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-f3800",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX F3800 Portable Power Station",
    "category": "3,840Wh LFP, 6,000W, 120/240V",
    "badge": "Best Anker for Home Backup",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "6,000W with native 120/240V output",
      "Runs 240V loads such as many well pumps",
      "Expands with BP3800 batteries to a very large system",
      "Pairs with Anker's home power panel",
      "Five-year warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 132 pounds",
      "Circuit-level backup needs a licensed electrician",
      "Overkill for anything short of home backup"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Anker to buy for whole-home essentials and 240V loads; pair it with a transfer switch and solar.",
    "description": "The F3800 is the pick when the constraint is output rather than storage. Anker rates it at 3,840 watt-hours of LFP storage and 6,000 watts of continuous output with native 120/240V, which is more inverter headroom than most portable stations offer and the reason it suits homes with a 240V well pump or other heavy motor loads. Connected through a transfer switch, an interlock or Anker's home power panel, it can run a refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump, internet and lights for roughly a day, longer with solar. BP3800 expansion batteries stack to a total capacity in the mid-twenties of kilowatt-hours, which moves it into territory normally occupied by wall-mounted home batteries. It weighs about 132 pounds and rolls on wheels, so think of it as a home appliance rather than a portable. Have a licensed electrician handle the connection, and confirm your pump's starting current before relying on it."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-f3800-plus",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus Portable Power Station",
    "category": "3,840Wh LFP, 6,000W, Higher Solar Input",
    "badge": "Best Anker for Big Solar",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Same 6,000W 120/240V output as the F3800",
      "Higher solar input ceiling for large arrays",
      "Faster recharging than the original, per Anker",
      "Expandable for long outages"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more than the original F3800",
      "Extra solar headroom is wasted without a large array"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Choose it over the F3800 only if you plan a large solar array; otherwise the original is the better value.",
    "description": "The F3800 Plus keeps everything that makes the F3800 the best Anker for home backup, including 3,840 watt-hours of LFP storage and 6,000 watts of 120/240V output by Anker's rating, and focuses the upgrade on charging. Anker's spec sheet lists a higher solar input ceiling than the original, which matters if you plan a large fixed or ground-mounted array: more panels can feed the battery at once, so it refills faster in the hours of strong sun you actually get during a multi-day outage. Recharging overall is quicker as well. That is the whole case for it. If you plan to run a couple of portable panels, or mostly recharge from the grid between outages, the extra headroom does nothing for you and the original F3800 is the better value. If you are building a serious solar-plus-battery setup that should carry the house through several days, the Plus is the one to buy."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Anker Power Station",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-anker-solix-power-station/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best Anker power station for most people?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The SOLIX C1000. Anker rates it at 1,056Wh and 1,800W, enough to keep a refrigerator, internet and lights going through a typical outage, and it refills from the wall in under an hour. It expands to about 2kWh with the BP1000 and is still light enough for car camping. The C1000 Gen 2 is a worthy upgrade if priced close."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Anker SOLIX the same as Anker PowerHouse?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "SOLIX is Anker's current brand for power stations and home batteries, and it replaced the PowerHouse name. Some models were simply renamed, such as the PowerHouse 767 becoming the SOLIX F2000. Older PowerHouse units with NMC cells were not carried over, and those are best avoided, because current SOLIX models use longer-lasting LFP cells."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can the Anker SOLIX F3800 power a whole house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can power the essentials, not everything at once. With 3,840Wh and 6,000W at 120/240V, it can run a fridge, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump, lights and internet for roughly a day through a transfer switch or Anker's home power panel. Central air conditioning, electric ranges and electric water heaters will drain it far too quickly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will an Anker C1000 run a refrigerator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A typical modern refrigerator uses roughly 1 to 2kWh per day, cycling on and off. After inverter losses, the C1000's 1,056Wh delivers somewhere around 900Wh of usable AC energy, so expect most of a day for an efficient fridge, less for an older or larger one. Adding the BP1000 battery roughly doubles that."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between the C1000 and C1000X?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The C1000X is generally a retailer-specific variant of the C1000 with the same core battery and inverter, sometimes with a different color or bundle. Compare the capacity, output and included accessories on each listing; if they match, buy whichever is cheaper or available sooner. Do not confuse either with the C1000 Gen 2, which is a different design."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I take an Anker power station on a plane?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Airlines generally limit carry-on lithium batteries to 100Wh, or up to 160Wh with approval. Even the smallest SOLIX power station, the C300X at 288Wh, exceeds that limit. For travel, choose an Anker portable power bank under 100Wh instead, and leave the power station at home or ship it ground by an approved carrier."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What warranty does Anker offer on SOLIX power stations?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Anker offers a five-year warranty on its current SOLIX power stations, which is among the longer warranties in the category and matches or beats most rivals. Coverage terms vary by model and region, and some apply only to units bought from authorized sellers, so register the product and keep the receipt from a recognized retailer."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Power & Generators",
    tags: [
      "best anker power station",
      "anker powerstation",
      "anker battery backup",
      "anker solix",
      "anker power bank portable",
      "anker solix f3800",
      "anker solix c1000"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          The Best Anker Power Station for the Job
        </h1>
        <QuickAnswer products={PRODUCTS} />
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          Anker sells a SOLIX for everything from a weekend of phone charging to a 240V home backup system. Here is which one to buy, which to skip, and why the model names are more confusing than the products.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          We judge every product on its spec sheet, its label and what owners report over time, not on the marketing. No paid placements, and commission never sets a ranking. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">11 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#lineup" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>The Lineup in One Minute</span></a>
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>The Seven Picks</span></a>
              <a href="#compare" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>Side-by-Side Comparison</span></a>
              <a href="#naming" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>Decoding the Model Names</span></a>
              <a href="#charging" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>Charging and Solar</span></a>
              <a href="#home-backup" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Anker Battery Backup at Home</span></a>
              <a href="#power-banks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Power Bank or Power Station?</span></a>
              <a href="#rivals" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Anker vs. the Rivals</span></a>
              <a href="#which" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Which Anker to Buy</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Anker's power stations are fast to charge, built on LFP cells and backed by one of the longer warranties in the category. The hard part is not deciding whether Anker is good; it is working out which of a dozen similar names you actually need. For most people the answer is the SOLIX C1000, and the rest of this guide explains when it is not.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Anker SOLIX Lineup in One Minute
          </h2>

          <p>
            Anker is better known for phone chargers and power banks than for big batteries, and that history shows in its power stations in a good way. The company leans hard on charging speed, compact cases and sensible port layouts, and it moved its whole lineup to lithium iron phosphate (LFP) cells earlier than some rivals. Everything it sells in this category now carries the SOLIX name, which replaced the older PowerHouse branding, so you will still see listings like the PowerHouse 767 that are the same box as the SOLIX F2000.
          </p>

          <p>
            The range splits into two families. The C series covers portable units you can carry by hand, from the roughly 300 watt-hour C300X to the roughly 1 kilowatt-hour C1000 and its second-generation follow-up. The F series covers the heavy, wheeled units built for home backup and RV use, topped by the F3800 and F3800 Plus with native 120/240V output. Between them sits the C800 Plus, a camping-focused unit with a built-in light. Anker also sells expansion batteries for several models and a home power panel for the F3800, which is where the brand starts competing with whole-home battery systems.
          </p>

          <p>
            The useful way to shop the lineup is by job, not by size. If your goal is charging phones, laptops and a camera on a weekend, a small C-series unit is plenty and anything larger is weight you will regret. If your goal is keeping a refrigerator, a modem and some lights going through an outage, you are in the 1kWh class, and that is where Anker is strongest. If your goal is running a well pump, a furnace blower and a freezer for a day, you need the F3800 and a transfer switch, and the conversation becomes about installation as much as batteries.
          </p>

          <p>
