          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best Anker power station for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The SOLIX C1000. Anker rates it at 1,056Wh and 1,800W, enough to keep a refrigerator, internet and lights going through a typical outage, and it refills from the wall in under an hour. It expands to about 2kWh with the BP1000 and is still light enough for car camping. The C1000 Gen 2 is a worthy upgrade if priced close.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Anker SOLIX the same as Anker PowerHouse?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                SOLIX is Anker's current brand for power stations and home batteries, and it replaced the PowerHouse name. Some models were simply renamed, such as the PowerHouse 767 becoming the SOLIX F2000. Older PowerHouse units with NMC cells were not carried over, and those are best avoided, because current SOLIX models use longer-lasting LFP cells.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can the Anker SOLIX F3800 power a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can power the essentials, not everything at once. With 3,840Wh and 6,000W at 120/240V, it can run a fridge, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump, lights and internet for roughly a day through a transfer switch or Anker's home power panel. Central air conditioning, electric ranges and electric water heaters will drain it far too quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will an Anker C1000 run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical modern refrigerator uses roughly 1 to 2kWh per day, cycling on and off. After inverter losses, the C1000's 1,056Wh delivers somewhere around 900Wh of usable AC energy, so expect most of a day for an efficient fridge, less for an older or larger one. Adding the BP1000 battery roughly doubles that.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the C1000 and C1000X?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The C1000X is generally a retailer-specific variant of the C1000 with the same core battery and inverter, sometimes with a different color or bundle. Compare the capacity, output and included accessories on each listing; if they match, buy whichever is cheaper or available sooner. Do not confuse either with the C1000 Gen 2, which is a different design.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take an Anker power station on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Airlines generally limit carry-on lithium batteries to 100Wh, or up to 160Wh with approval. Even the smallest SOLIX power station, the C300X at 288Wh, exceeds that limit. For travel, choose an Anker portable power bank under 100Wh instead, and leave the power station at home or ship it ground by an approved carrier.
              </p>
